TY  - JOUR
TI  - Analgesic efficacy of external oblique intercostal plane block in adult upper abdominal laparoscopic surgery: a systematic review and meta-analysis of randomized controlled trials.
AU  - Azem Karam
AU  - Fein Shai
AU  - Aranbitski Roussana
AU  - Orbach-Zinger Sharon
AU  - Ronen Ariel
AU  - Binyamin Yair
AU  - Kanani Fahim
AU  - Heesen Philip
JO  - Brazilian journal of anesthesiology (Elsevier)
PY  - 2026 Nov 18
DO  - 10.1016/j.bjane.2026.844863
AB  - BACKGROUND: The External Oblique Intercostal (EOI) plane block is a novel regional anesthesia technique for upper abdominal surgery. Existing meta-analyses have pooled heterogeneous surgical populations or grouped comparators together, leaving its efficacy relative to individual fascial plane blocks unclear. METHODS: We searched Embase, PubMed, and CENTRAL for RCTs up to December 24, 2025, comparing EOI plane block with control, Local Anesthetic (LA) infiltration, or other fascial plane blocks in adults undergoing upper abdominal laparoscopic surgery. Primary outcomes were pain at rest and on movement at 12- and 24-hours; secondary outcomes were opioid consumption and quality of recovery at 24-hours. Risk of bias and certainty of evidence were assessed. RESULTS: Eleven RCTs (695 participants) were included. EOI plane block significantly reduced pain compared with control at all time points (mean differences -0.88 to -1.14), exceeding the Minimal Clinically Important Difference (MCID) only for pain on movement at 24-hours (-1.14; 95% CI -1.43, -0.84). Pain was also reduced versus LA infiltration for most outcomes but remained below the MCID. No significant differences were observed versus Subcostal Transversus Abdominis Plane (STAP) block or Modified Thoracoabdominal nerve block through Perichondrial Approach (MTAPA) for any outcome. Opioid consumption and quality of recovery did not differ in any comparison. Certainty of evidence was low or very low for most comparisons. CONCLUSION: EOI plane block may reduce postoperative pain compared with control and LA infiltration, although most differences fell below the MCID, with no demonstrated advantage over STAP block or MTAPA for any outcome.
