TI  - Ultrasound-guided costotransverse block with systemic adjuvants for complete opioid-free breast cancer surgery: A randomized controlled trial.
AU  - Banish T M
AU  - Sethi Priyanka
AU  - Mathur Rhythm
AU  - Dang Deepanshu
AU  - Kaur Manbir
AU  - Kumari Kamlesh
AU  - Sharma Nivedita
AU  - Bhatia Pradeep Kumar
JO  - Journal of anaesthesiology, clinical pharmacology
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.4103/joacp.joacp_746_25
AB  - BACKGROUND AND AIMS: Modified radical mastectomy (MRM) is associated with significant postoperative pain requiring opioid analgesia. Opioids carry undesirable effects including nausea, sedation, respiratory depression, opioid-induced hyperalgesia, and potential immune suppression that may impact oncological outcomes. This randomized controlled trial assessed the effectiveness of opioid-free anesthesia using ultrasound-guided costotransverse block (CTB) with multimodal systemic adjuvants on analgesic outcomes and recovery quality in breast cancer surgery. MATERIAL AND METHODS: This prospective, open-label, randomized controlled trial was conducted at tertiary care center with institutional ethics approval. Sixty-six female patients (American society of anesthesiologist physical staus grade(ASA) I-III) undergoing MRM were randomly allocated (1:1) to opioid-based anesthesia (Group A, n = 33) or opioid-free anesthesia with T4-level CTB using 0.5% ropivacaine (15 mL) plus dexmedetomidine (0.5 µg/kg/h), lidocaine (1.5 mg/kg bolus, 2 mg/kg/h infusion), and magnesium sulfate (30 mg/kg over 20 minutes) (Group B, RESULTS: Complete elimination of perioperative opioid requirements was achieved in Group B. Intraoperative fentanyl consumption in Group B was 0 µg vs Group A 150 (132.5-150) µg ( CONCLUSIONS: Ultrasound-guided CTB with multimodal systemic adjuvants provided complete elimination of perioperative opioid requirements while achieving superior pain control, enhanced recovery quality, and excellent safety in MRM. This opioid-free approach offers a clinically viable alternative to conventional opioid-based techniques. This approach can be a safe inclusion in enhanced recovery protocols for breast cancer surgery.
